 "standing": "Proposed source and checks complete for root's six checkpoint-2 sub-findings. Source editing STOPPED pending root-review.v3.json. Coauthor technical review; not independent acceptance, not blind acceptance, not user approval, not readiness, not product qualification.",
 "sourceRoot": "/tmp/opensip-design-corrections/bv4-corrections-author.v3/work",
 "subFindingDispositions": [
  {
   "id": "CX-BV4-DEFAULT-PUBLIC-CARRIER/1 multi-step bound",
   "position": "AGREE - my no-truncation proof was false",
   "whatWasWrong": "1024 is the ANALYSIS-SPEC requestedCapabilities bound, not an invocation-wide one. A named multi-step invocation carries up to 64 steps, so two admitted selections of 1023 each compose 2046 notices and the flat 1024 array refused. Root's probe reproduced exactly that.",
   "correction": "Composition is now PER STEP: CapabilityAvailabilityStepV1 {stepId, noticeCount, notices[<=1024]} and CapabilityAvailabilityV1 {stepCount, totalNoticeCount, steps[<=64]}, bounded by the invocation's own StepId range. No notice is discarded to fit and no invocation-wide request cap is imposed.",
   "lifecycleLaws": "a step that made no selection contributes NO entry; a step that selected and found nothing absent contributes an EMPTY entry, which is the positive statement that it checked; a retried step contributes one entry for the attempt the invocation retained; the same ownership tuple may recur in different steps, so uniqueness is within a step and never across the invocation.",
   "verified": [
    "two-admitted-selections-exceeding-1024-combined-still-compose (1023+1023=2046, ADMIT)",
    "the-same-notices-flattened-into-one-step-would-refuse-the-analysis-spec-bound",
    "each-availability-step-is-associated-with-its-owning-step-id",
    "a-one-step-invocation-projects-a-single-availability-step",
    "a-step-that-selected-and-found-nothing-keeps-an-empty-entry",
    "a-step-that-made-no-selection-contributes-no-entry",
    "the-same-ownership-tuple-may-recur-in-different-steps",
    "the-total-count-is-the-sum-of-the-step-counts"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "CX-BV4-DEFAULT-PUBLIC-CARRIER/2 renderer parity",
   "position": "AGREE - envelope membership is not parity",
   "whatWasWrong": "render selects only a command's declared parityFields, so the collection reached JSON and agent and was omitted by human, SARIF and HTML. Being on the envelope proved nothing about propagation.",
   "correction": "`capability-availability` is a declared parityField of the analysis commands - default, analyze, fit, audit - in workflows/command-inventory.v1.json, and render projects it for every applicable format. The key is distinct from any existing query/purge availability meaning.",
   "verified": [
    "the-analysis-commands-declare-capability-availability-as-a-parity-field",
    "the-declared-parity-field-reaches-every-applicable-renderer"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "CX-BV4-DEFAULT-PUBLIC-CARRIER/3 notice code",
   "position": "AGREE - confirmed",
   "correction": "CapabilityAvailabilityNoticeV1.code is now `const native.capability-unavailable` rather than the whole DomainDetailCode enum, because the typed record declares exactly one condition.",
   "verified": [
    "a-release-absence-notice-cannot-carry-a-foreign-detail-code (CONFIG.INVALID now REFUSES)",
    "the-notice-code-is-a-const-not-the-whole-detail-enum"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "CX-BV4-PUBLIC-ROUTE-COMPLETE/1 overbound subject",
   "position": "AGREE - confirmed",
   "whatWasWrong": "a structurally valid 4096-character languageMode produced a 4149-character subject in all three origin routes, and the full failure envelope refused BoundedText maxLength 1024.",
   "correction": "bounded_subject elides: the registered KEY is preserved verbatim - it selects the code, class and origin - and only the offending value is elided, keeping a leading window plus the full SHA-256 of the untruncated subject so two long values never collide and the retained diagnostic can be matched. Never schema-invalid, never a dropped subject, never reclassified as success.",
   "verified": [
    "an-overbound-refused-value-is-elided-not-emitted-whole (4149 -> exactly 1024, in all three origins)",
    "an-overbound-value-still-composes-an-admitted-envelope-in-every-origin",
    "elision-preserves-the-code-class-and-origin-and-never-reclassifies-as-success",
    "two-different-overbound-values-do-not-collide-after-elision",
    "exact-bound and overbound inputs both exercised through the actual guard"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "CX-BV4-PUBLIC-ROUTE-COMPLETE/2 preview guard and probe honesty",
   "position": "AGREE on both",
   "whatWasWrong": "the preview-* release guard raised a prose sentence that the normalizer correctly refused, so an explicitly advertised invalid-release-declaration path had no envelope. AND my p2_envelope.py labelled a hardcoded array `REAL guard outputs` while never invoking a guard - they were representative-string composition tests, and the label was wrong.",
   "correction": "the guard now raises native.release-capability-preview-constant:<id>, a registered key routed to the release-declaration envelope. p2_envelope.py is relabelled as a composition test in its own text, and a new probe plus a new control drive TEN actual guard invocations through normalization, termination and full envelope - which is how a prose-emitting guard is caught.",
   "verified": [
    "every-ACTUAL-guard-refusal-composes-a-schema-admitted-failure-envelope",
    "the-preview-release-guard-emits-a-registered-key-not-prose",
    "no-actual-capability-guard-emits-an-unregistered-prose-refusal",
    "every-representative-guard-string-composes-a-schema-admitted-failure-envelope (renamed honestly)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "CX-BV4-PUBLIC-ROUTE-COMPLETE/3 D9 mirrors",
   "position": "AGREE - confirmed",
   "correction": "common.schema.json D9FaultCause.description and the workflows_model FAULT_TO_ERROR comment now state the narrow law: the host-INVARIANT subtype specifically was unrepresentable, other host-fault causes already existed, `total and injective` is a property of the declared cause domain so this does NOT make the maps total, and faultCause does grow by one member. workflows-and-surfaces section 9's cause enumeration includes host-invariant. Historical v1.14 bytes unchanged.",
   "verified": [
    "the-prose-does-not-claim-the-inherited-maps-were-nontotal",
    "the-selected-d9-composition-adds-exactly-one-fault-cause-and-no-code",
    "every-inherited-fault-mapping-is-preserved-exactly",
    "the-historical-d9-artifact-bytes-are-unchanged"
   ]
  }
 ],
